    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nwss_wval#9",
      "doc_id": "nwss_wval",
      "heading": "Using the Wastewater Viral Activity Level > Understanding the WVAL > How it is calculated > Step 6: Categorize values",
      "text": "Group the WVAL values into one of five categories (see below for thresholds): Very low, low, moderate, high, or very high. These categories provide a quick understanding of the levels.\n\n**Note:** Each virus has its own thresholds because each virus behaves differently**.** For example, a WVAL value of 10.0 would align with moderate influenza A levels, high COVID-19 levels, and very high RSV levels."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "nwss_national#4",
      "doc_id": "nwss_national",
      "heading": "National Wastewater Data for Respiratory Viruses > About the data > Wastewater viral activity level > Wastewater viral activity level categories",
      "text": "| Regional Tourism Highlights and Health Risks in Mexico - Table 11.2.3.1 |  |  |  |  |  | \n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|  | **Very Low** | **Low** | **Moderate** | **High** | **Very High** | \n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Influenza A** | Up to 2.4 | Greater than 2.4 and up to 5.5 | Greater than 5.5 and up to 10.2 | Greater than 10.2 and up to 15.6 | Greater than 15.6 | \n| **COVID-19** | Up to 2.6 | Greater than 2.6 and up to 4.9 | Greater than 4.9 and up to 7.9 | Greater than 7.9 and up to 11.6 | Greater than 11.6 | \n| **RSV** | Up to 1.7 | Greater than 1.7 and up to 3.4 | Greater than 3.4 and up to 5.4 | Greater than 5.4 and up to 8.1 | Greater than 8.1 |"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#6",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Wastewater viral activity level",
      "text": "- Wastewater monitoring data are not available in all communities or in areas that are not connected to a wastewater treatment system.\n- The wastewater viral activity level (WVAL) is available for the virus that causes COVID-19 (SARS-COV-2), Influenza A, and RSV present in samples of wastewater taken from sites across the United States. More detailed methods can be found in About Wastewater Data | National Wastewater Surveillance System | CDC."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#17",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Display criteria for sub-state levels (categories)",
      "text": "RSV and COVID-19 are limited to nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs), also listed as polymerase chain rection tests (PCR). Participating laboratories report weekly to CDC the total number of COVID-19, flu, and RSV tests performed that week and the number of those tests that were positive.\n\nThe map shows the Wastewater Viral Activity Level across US states and territories. Activity levels indicate how much virus is currently detected in the wastewater \u2013 ranging from very low to very high. Wastewater monitoring can detect viruses spreading from one person to another within a community earlier than clinical testing, and before people who are sick go to their healthcare provider or hospital. It can also detect infections in people without symptoms. For more information, see Data Methods.\n\nData representing less than 5% of the population has a hatching, or lined, pattern on the map, indicating the wastewater viral activity level may not show the full picture for that state or territory. States or territories without sufficient data are indicated as \"No data\". This can be because data was not reported from the state or territory, or the monitored sites do not have enough data to calculate the wastewater viral activity level."
    }
  ]
}

